    <output type="test">tests/unit/Sprk.Bff.Api.Tests/Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/AccessibleRecordSetServiceTests.cs</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">A contact actively in org X derives membership of a record referencing X via a REGISTRY-LISTED org-typed lookup, contributing at X's baseline rights (org holds standing grant, baseline View Only ⇒ exactly Read).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">FR-24 negative: the same record referencing org X ONLY via a non-registry lookup (opposing counsel) yields NO org-derived contribution — the column is absent from the emitted FetchXml.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative: an INACTIVE junction row (statecode ≠ 0) confers nothing; a contact in no orgs gets zero org-derived entries.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">FR-22 negative: on a secure record, the org-derived contribution is suppressed pre-max for BOTH a ContactOnly principal and a Type 1 systemuser whose linked contact is in org X.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Max interaction: org-derived ViewOnly + direct Collaborate grant on the same record ⇒ Read|Write|Create.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Systemuser fallback: ComposeForSystemUserAsync (impersonation flag OFF) passes AccessConferringOnly=true — an adverse-lookup-derived record no longer enters the composed set (A-8 closure pinned by test); the flag-ON branch byte-unchanged.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative: junction or org-baseline read fault ⇒ no org-derived contribution, warning logged, gate does not 500.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Build green; publish size reported and ≤60 MB.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Design §4.5 terms 3-4; spec FR-24/FR-25/FR-22; register B-1/B-12; investigation 02 §3/§4.2. The standing-gated implementation choice for the org term is deliberately conservative pending the escalation's owner answer — record the outcome in notes/ and in the PR.</notes>

  <completion date="2026-09-17" commit="28f833a0e">
    Delivered design §4.5 term 4 (org expansion, standing-gated at the ORGANISATION's baseline) and
    wired FR-24's registry filter into the systemuser membership path, closing the register A-8
    asymmetry the contact plane never had.

    PREREQUISITE THE BRIEF DID NOT NAME: MembershipResolverService.HashOptions omitted
    AccessConferringOnly, so setting it true here would have made the AUTHORIZATION caller and the
    SCOPING caller (MembershipEndpoints) share one 5-minute cache entry for the same
    user+entity+limit. A scoping call landing first hands the gate the UNFILTERED descriptor set —
    A-8 restored through Redis instead of through code. Latent only because task 041 deliberately
    left the flag unset everywhere. Both that flag and the new OrganizationIds are now hashed.

    DESIGN DECISIONS (taken, not escalated): the evaluator resolves the junction ONCE and passes the
    org ids inward, rather than the resolver reading them (which would invert the layering and add a
    second cache that could disagree mid-composition); the read returns an OUTCOME, not a list,
    because the additive term and the FR-23 deny-veto subject fail in OPPOSITE directions and an
    empty list would have silently turned the veto's fail-CLOSED into a fail-OPEN; one walk per
    DISTINCT baseline (≤3), because one walk for all organisations could credit only a single level
    and a record reachable solely through a View Only firm would inherit a Full Access firm's rights
    undetectably; the org walk narrows to IdentityTypes ["Organization"] so the always-bound
    ContactId cannot drag contact-derived records in at an organisation's level.

    ESCALATION TRIGGER 1 DID NOT FIRE — the standing-gated shape this task was told to encode is what
    shipped, and no non-standing derived term exists today. TRIGGER 2 DID NOT FIRE — no
    MembershipResponse field was added, so the M10 wire shape is unchanged.

    TWO TASK-020 CONSTRAINTS DECIDED AND RECORDED EITHER WAY (notes §4): the sprk_enddate read-side
    asymmetry is deliberately NOT bounded, because QueryActiveOrgIdsAsync is shared by an ADDITIVE
    caller and a VETO subject whose fail directions are inverted — one query shape cannot be right
    for both, and a blanket date bound would make the ethical wall match FEWER subjects. Filed as an
    owner item; exposure is currently nil (0 organisations hold a standing grant). Per-member cache
    invalidation is also NOT added, for a different reason than 020's: the staleness that matters is
    the resolver's own 5-minute response cache, which a junction-row invalidation would not touch —
    ADR-034's Phase 2 pub/sub invalidator is the mechanism that actually closes it.

    POML PREMISE ERRORS 18 AND 19: it cites ContactStandingGrantReader.cs, which task 042 renamed
    SubjectStandingGrantReader.cs; and step 3 says wire the flag into "the flag-off branch of 036",
    but 036 is open (gated behind 034) and no such branch exists — the single ResolveAsync call IS
    the path. Its three dependency status attributes were also stale. Separately, ResolveByContactAsync's
    own XML doc still described the RETIRED sprk_assigned* convention as the allowlist (task 041
    deleted it); corrected in place — 12th docs-vs-reality instance.

    REPLACED task 042's deliberate scope-boundary test (Times.Never on ReadForOrganizationAsync,
    reason "task 043 owns it") with the closed set it was marking — inverted, not deleted.

    STEP 9.5 CRITICAL C-1 — ESCALATED, THEN IMPLEMENTED ON THE OWNER'S DECISION (2026-09-17):
    "implement the correct technical and user access control option; note that records will be
    primarily owned at the team/business unit and not individual users". Turning on
    AccessConferringOnly did not only close A-8 — it also DELETED owner/team/business-unit membership
    from the systemuser plane, because the conferring registry can only express Contact/Organization
    columns (FilterToAccessConferringRoles rejects anything else as malformed), making ownerid
    INEXPRESSIBLE rather than merely unregistered. This task's OWN test had asserted the regression as
    correct (it seeded ownerid and expected roles == {assignedAttorney}), which is how it passed
    review-by-author. It had also already happened in production: R7 W12 task 130 recorded
    "sprk_matter resolved rows=0 for a user who owns 44 matters via ownerid, verified via raw SQL".

    FIX: ownerid / owningteam / owningbusinessunit confer STRUCTURALLY, ahead of the registry and
    without an entry. FR-24 exists so a MAKER-AUTHORED lookup cannot confer by naming accident; these
    three are platform-maintained and fixed by the Dataverse data model, so there is nothing for a
    review to protect, and being the owner IS Dataverse access — the thing this filter approximates
    until task 036 lands. NARROW by construction: keyed on the three platform NAMES, never the
    identity type, so a maker-authored sprk_reviewer -> systemuser still needs a reviewed entry.
    owningteam is the load-bearing column and ownerid alone would have been a FALSE fix: discovery
    binds the first target matching IncludedIdentityTables, which starts at systemuser, so a
    polymorphic Owner column always resolves to SystemUser and binds the caller's own id — on a
    team-owned record ownerid holds the TEAM's id and never matches.
    ADR-034 Amendment A1.1 applied (CLAUDE.md 6.5 path B, owner-approved).

    I OVER-WIDENED IT FIRST AND A PRE-EXISTING TEST CAUGHT ME: applied unconditionally the allowance
    reached the contact plane, where ResolveByContactAsync_AllowlistedAssignedContactRole_
    ReturnsMatchingRecords failed with ByRole {["owner"] = {empty}, ...} against its own NFR-05 reason.
    A contact cannot own a Dataverse record, so those descriptors bound nothing and merely widened the
    query. Fixed by threading includePlatformOwnership per call site (true on systemuser, false on
    contact), keeping the contact plane byte-identical. The test's expectations were NOT touched.

    VERIFICATION: BFF build 0/0 · edited classes 111/111 with every filter proven non-empty before
    being trusted · affected namespaces 331/331 · seam + auth surfaces 197/197 (189 selected),
    including all three StandingGrantRuntimeUnionSeamTests methods by name · ArchTests 323/323 ·
    perturbations 13/13 CAUGHT (11 core + 2 for the C-1 platform-ownership fix) · publish 45.45 MB vs
    re-measured master 45.35 = +0.10 MB, 214 files each side, re-measured at the final commit.
    C1-b is the perturbation worth having: it recreates the exact over-widening committed in the first
    cut, and the test that fails is the PRE-EXISTING NFR-05 assertion — so that invariant is genuinely
    guarded rather than having caught me once by luck.

    A VERIFICATION ERROR WORTH CARRYING FORWARD (notes §8): a filtered run reported "Failed: 0,
    Passed: 16" for a filter that selected the WRONG 16 — tests/integration/{auth,seam}/** are
    globbed into the UNIT csproj, not the integration one, so the seam test was never in the run. A
    filter selecting nothing reports identically to one selecting everything. Prove the filter
    non-empty before trusting the result.
  </completion>

